==============================================================================
H044 -- THE SATELLITE PREDICTION: PHANTOM TRUNCATION IN A HOST FIELD
==============================================================================

  a_0 footing 1 = 9.3619e-11 m/s^2
  a_0 footing 2 = 1.1279e-10 m/s^2
  a_0 from 0.5 c sqrt(G rho_L) = 9.3624e-11 m/s^2  (printed, not used as a gate)
  Omega_dm/Omega_b = 5.4082  ->  <g_ext> = 0.1849 a_0   (H021/A3)
  host baryon mass (fiducial MW) = 6.000e+10 M_sun

==============================================================================
PART 1 -- THE CAP RADIUS OF A SATELLITE:  r_cap = D sqrt(M_sat/M_host)
==============================================================================

  H021/A2:  r_cap = r_M * (a_0/g_ext)
  deep host: g_host(D) = sqrt(a_0 G M_h)/D = a_0 r_M,h / D
  => r_cap = sqrt(G M_s a_0/a_0) * D / r_M,h
           = D * sqrt(G M_s / a_0) / sqrt(G M_h / a_0)
           = D * sqrt(M_s / M_h)                       <-- a_0 CANCELS

  footing                 r_M,host [kpc]   D* = 5.408 r_M,h
  f1 (9.3619e-11)                  9.453              51.12
  f2 (1.1279e-10)                  8.612              46.58
  [PASS] S1 [THE CAP IS a_0-FREE] r_cap = D sqrt(M_sat/M_host): the two footings
      must agree exactly, because a_0 cancels between r_M and g_ext
         measured:  |r_cap(f1) - r_cap(f2)|/r_cap = 0.000e+00  (D=100 kpc, M_sat=1e7, M_h=6e10 -> r_cap = 1.2910 kpc)
         threshold: relative difference < 1e-12 (machine agreement)
         THIS IS THE SHARPEST STRUCTURAL RESULT IN THE LANE. The satellite's halo
         truncation radius is independent of a_0, of G, and of the footing:
         it is distance times sqrt(baryon mass ratio). It is therefore
         testable WITHOUT knowing a_0 -- a rare thing in this framework.

  the master identity (measurement over a grid):
       M_sat [Msun]  D [kpc]  r_cap/r_M,sat   D/r_M,host    ratio
            1.0e+02       20         2.1158       2.1158  1.00000
            1.0e+02       60         6.3474       6.3474  1.00000
            1.0e+02      150        15.8685      15.8685  1.00000
            1.0e+02      300        31.7371      31.7371  1.00000
            1.0e+04       20         2.1158       2.1158  1.00000
            1.0e+04       60         6.3474       6.3474  1.00000
            1.0e+04      150        15.8685      15.8685  1.00000
            1.0e+04      300        31.7371      31.7371  1.00000
            1.0e+06       20         2.1158       2.1158  1.00000
            1.0e+06       60         6.3474       6.3474  1.00000
            1.0e+06      150        15.8685      15.8685  1.00000
            1.0e+06      300        31.7371      31.7371  1.00000
            1.0e+08       20         2.1158       2.1158  1.00000
            1.0e+08       60         6.3474       6.3474  1.00000
            1.0e+08      150        15.8685      15.8685  1.00000
            1.0e+08      300        31.7371      31.7371  1.00000
  [PASS] S2 [THE MASTER IDENTITY] r_cap/r_M,sat = D/r_M,host over 4 decades of
      satellite mass and 20-300 kpc: the satellite's own mass cancels
         measured:  max |lhs/rhs - 1| over the 16-cell grid = 2.220e-16
         threshold: max relative deviation < 1e-9
         CONSEQUENCE: a satellite's dark-to-baryon ratio is set ONLY by how many
         HOST MOND radii out it sits -- not by its own mass. That is a
         falsifiable statement: two satellites of very different stellar
         mass at the same host-centric distance must show the SAME
         M_dyn/M_star.

==============================================================================
PART 2 -- THE UNIFIED SIGMA LAW AND ITS ISOLATED LIMIT
==============================================================================

  M_dyn(<R) = M_b [1 + min(R, r_cap)/r_M]            (H021/A1 + A2)
  adopt the coefficient that the ISOLATED limit fixes (must reproduce G070):
      sigma_los^2 = (G M_b / 2R) * [1 + min(R/r_M , D/r_M,h)]
  R >> r_M, uncapped:  sigma^2 -> (G M_b/2 r_M) = (1/2) sqrt(G M_b a_0)
      =>  sigma = (G M_b a_0)^{1/4} / sqrt(2)        [G070, certified]

      footing                   R/r_M  sigma_law/sigma_iso
      f1 (9.3619e-11)              10         1.0488088482
      f1 (9.3619e-11)             100         1.0049875621
      f1 (9.3619e-11)           10000         1.0000499988
      f1 (9.3619e-11)         1000000         1.0000005000
      f2 (1.1279e-10)              10         1.0488088482
      f2 (1.1279e-10)             100         1.0049875621
      f2 (1.1279e-10)           10000         1.0000499988
      f2 (1.1279e-10)         1000000         1.0000005000
  [PASS] S3 [THE LIMIT] the unified law must reduce to G070's certified isolated
      line (G M_b a_0)^{1/4}/sqrt(2) when the cap does not bite
         measured:  |sigma_law/sigma_iso - 1| at R/r_M = 1e6: f1=5.00e-07  f2=5.00e-07
         threshold: relative deviation < 1e-3 for both footings
         So the law is not a new normalisation: it is G070's line with a cap
         welded on, and it reproduces G070 exactly where the cap is inert.

==============================================================================
PART 3 -- THE SUPPRESSION FACTOR AND ITS DISTANCE DEPENDENCE
==============================================================================

  Compare a satellite at D to a FIELD galaxy of the same baryon mass, whose
  external field is the cosmic mean <g_ext> = 0.1849 a_0 (H021/A3), i.e. a
  field cap ratio of a_0/g_ext = 5.408.

      x  ==  R_1/2 / r_M,sat                       (how deep the satellite is)
      S_M(D) = [1 + min(x, D/r_M,h)] / [1 + min(x, 5.408)]     (mass)
      S_sig  = sqrt(S_M)                                        (velocity)

  LIMITS:   D -> 0        S_M -> 1/6.408 = 0.156      (max suppression 6.4x)
            D = 5.408 r_M,h   S_M = 1.000             (the crossover D*)
            D >= x r_M,h  S_M saturates at (1+x)/(1+min(x,5.408))

  worked at x = R_1/2/r_M,sat = 7.77 (M_b = 1e6 M_sun, R_1/2 = 300 pc):
