           100    10.579          8.770            6.408   1.369    1.170
           150    15.869          8.770            6.408   1.369    1.170
           200    21.158          8.770            6.408   1.369    1.170
           250    26.448          8.770            6.408   1.369    1.170
           300    31.737          8.770            6.408   1.369    1.170
  [PASS] S4 [THE CROSSOVER] the framework does NOT predict blanket suppression:
      there is a radius D* = 5.408 r_M,host where satellite = field
         measured:  D* = f1:51.1 kpc  f2:46.6 kpc  (measured S_M at 20 kpc = 0.486, at 100 kpc = 1.369)
         threshold: S_M(20 kpc) < 1 AND S_M(100 kpc) > 1 (sign must flip)
         PHYSICAL READING: dark mass in this framework MEASURES how quiet the
         environment is. Beyond D* the host field is quieter than the
         cosmic mean, so a satellite there grows a BIGGER phantom than
         the average field galaxy. The premise 'satellites always have
         less dark mass' holds only inside D*.
  [PASS] S5 [THE SUPPRESSION IS BOUNDED] for an MW-mass host the strongest
      suppression available to any REAL satellite (innermost is
      Sagittarius at D ~ 18 kpc) is finite and small
         measured:  S_M(18 kpc) = 0.453 (mass) = 0.344 dex, S_sigma = 0.673 = 0.172 dex; absolute floor as D->0: S_M = 0.156 = 0.807 dex, S_sigma = 0.395 = 0.403 dex
         threshold: S_M(18 kpc) > 0.3 (i.e. less than 0.53 dex of suppression)
         This number is what any 'satellites are dark-poor' claim has to beat.
         It is NOT enough for too-big-to-fail (see S9).

