      the boundary objects move; the strongly capped ones (r_cap < 0.5 R_1/2)
      do not, so the verdict is set by the robust subset, not the boundary.
  [PASS] S6 [WHERE THE FRAMEWORK SAYS THE CAP IS INERT, IT WORKS] the 8 uncapped
      satellites must sit on G070's zero-parameter isolated line
         measured:  median |log10(sigma_pred/sigma_obs)| = 0.079 dex; median signed = +0.049 dex (n = 8)
         threshold: median |log10| <= 0.15 dex
         This is the control, and it is clean: Fornax, Sculptor, Leo I, Leo II,
         Carina, CVn I, CVn II, Leo V -- the objects the framework says
         are NOT truncated -- scatter by only 0.08 dex (20%) about a
         line with no free parameters. Better than G070's full-sample 0.222.
  [FAIL] S7 [WHERE THE FRAMEWORK SAYS THE CAP BITES, IT FAILS] the 24 capped
      satellites must ALSO sit within 0.30 dex of the capped prediction
         measured:  median log10(sigma_pred/sigma_obs) = -0.487 dex (observed is 3.1x ABOVE prediction); median |log10| = 0.487 dex (n = 24); robust subset (capped even at D+8.3 kpc): n = 21, median = -0.535 dex
         threshold: median |log10(sigma_pred/sigma_obs)| <= 0.30 dex
         THE LANE'S MAIN RESULT, AND IT IS NEGATIVE. The sample splits cleanly on
         the framework's OWN criterion: the 8 it leaves alone agree to
         0.08 dex, the 24 it truncates are off by 0.49 dex (3.1x), and the
         21 that stay truncated under the full +/-8.3 kpc distance swing
         are off by 0.54 dex. The amplitude law survives; the EFE-as-
         truncation prescription does not. Note the sign: the deficit is
         one-sided (sigma_obs too HIGH), so no choice of M/L or IMF can
         absorb it -- raising M_b helps both branches equally, and
         unresolved binaries add at most ~0.3 dex.

==============================================================================
PART 5 -- IS IT THE SMOOTH FIELD OR THE TIDE? (the alternative prescription)
==============================================================================

  A satellite is in free fall: in its own frame the UNIFORM part of the host
  field is not what strips it -- the GRADIENT is.  The competing truncation is
  the Jacobi radius, solved with the framework's own mass profiles:
      r_J = D * (M_sat(r_J) / (3 M_host(D)))^{1/3},   M(<r) = M_b (1 + r/r_M)

