         DECISION-FORCING. The two prescriptions differ by 24 objects out of 32.
         Tidal truncation leaves 31/32 on G070's certified line; smooth-
         field EFE truncation breaks 24/32 by 0.49 dex. If H021/A2 is to
         be kept for satellites, it needs the tidal form, not g_ext.

==============================================================================
PART 6 -- (c) DOES ANY OF THIS EXPLAIN MISSING SATELLITES OR TBTF?
==============================================================================
  the classical dSphs (log M* > 5.5, Sagittarius excluded as disrupting):
      name                r_cap/R1/2  S_sigma  suppression [dex]
      Ursa Minor               0.555    0.745              0.128
      Sextans                  0.591    0.769              0.114
      Draco                    0.980    0.990              0.004
      Carina                   1.223    1.000             -0.000
      Canes Venatici I         1.244    1.000             -0.000
      Sculptor                 2.079    1.000             -0.000
      Fornax                   3.778    1.000             -0.000
      Leo II                   5.589    1.000             -0.000
      Leo I                    9.873    1.000             -0.000
  [FAIL] S9 [TOO-BIG-TO-FAIL IS NOT SOLVED BY THE EFE] TBTF needs the satellites
      to be suppressed by a factor ~2-3 in V_max (0.30-0.50 dex);
      the EFE must be able to supply that for the classicals
         measured:  best suppression available among the 9 classicals = 0.128 dex (Ursa Minor, S_sigma = 0.745); median = -0.000 dex; absolute floor from S5 (D=18 kpc) = 0.172 dex; 5 of 9 have r_cap >= R_1/2 and get exactly 0.000 dex
         threshold: best available suppression >= 0.30 dex
         NO. Five of the nine classicals have r_cap >= R_1/2, so the EFE does
         nothing to them at all, and even the best case (Ursa Minor,
         r_cap/R_1/2 = 0.56) gives 0.13 dex -- a factor >= 2 short of what
         TBTF needs. The framework DISSOLVES TBTF structurally -- the
         phantom is a 1:1 response to baryons, so there is no independent
         halo-mass function that could be 'too big' -- but it does not
         SOLVE it via the external field.

  one phantom per baryon clump: predicted dark subhalos = 32, predicted luminous = 32, excess factor = 1.000
  the discrepancy that needs explaining is ~10x (LCDM dark subhalos vs observed luminous satellites)
  [FAIL] S10 [MISSING SATELLITES IS NOT ADDRESSED] the missing-satellites problem
      is a statement about the NUMBER of subhalos; the EFE must change a
      predicted COUNT, not merely a velocity dispersion
         measured:  EFE changes sigma for 24/32 satellites by 0.272 dex and leaves the predicted dark/luminous count ratio at 1.000 (needs >= 10 to even pose the problem, so there is nothing for the EFE to remove)
         threshold: the mechanism must alter a predicted satellite COUNT by >= 2x
         NO, and structurally so. The phantom is a response to whatever baryons
         exist; it is not a population of objects that can be counted and
         found wanting. What still needs baryonic astrophysics is exactly
         what needs it in LCDM: which low-mass halos formed stars at all
         (reionisation, feedback). The framework removes the DARK count
         problem and keeps the LUMINOUS one. That is a real gain, but it
         is not an EFE result and it is not a count prediction.

==============================================================================
PART 7 -- IS IT TESTABLE? THE THREE TESTS, RANKED
==============================================================================
  (1) THE BREAK IN sigma(R).  Beyond r_cap the enclosed mass stops growing,
      so sigma must fall as R^-1/2 instead of staying flat.
      r_cap for the classicals (pc): Ursa Minor=225, Sextans=270, Draco=226, Carina=380, Canes Venatici I=544, Sculptor=580, Fornax=2992, Leo II=956, Leo I=2666
      30/32 satellites have r_cap inside 0.1-2 kpc,
      the radial range published dispersion profiles already cover.
  (2) THE a_0-FREE SCALING  r_cap = D sqrt(M_sat/M_host): slope +1 in D,
      slope +1/2 in M_sat.  Measurable without knowing a_0.  The EFE
      prescription and the tidal prescription differ by a median factor
      r_J/r_cap = 26 in r_cap, so a single
      well-measured break separates them.
  (3) THE BIFURCATION. Already measured above: 0.08 dex vs 0.49 dex,
      split on the framework's own criterion, n = 8 vs 24.
  [PASS] S11 [TESTABLE] the truncation radius lands inside the measured radial
      range of published dSph dispersion profiles for most of the sample
         measured:  r_cap in [0.1, 2] kpc for 30/32 satellites; median r_cap = 58 pc; median r_J/r_cap = 25.6
         threshold: >= 20 of 32 inside the measured radial range
         Yes -- decisively. Tests (1) and (2) need only dispersion profiles that
         already exist: the classical dSphs have published sigma(R) out to
         of order 1 kpc (Walker et al. 2009, MNRAS 397, 1016 -- VERIFY
         that radial reach before quoting); predicted r_cap runs from
         225 pc (UMi/Draco) to 3.0 kpc (Fornax). Test (3) is already
         done here and has already returned its verdict.

